{"term":"bustle","senses":[{"id":"kaikki:ff6b68b71369f1e2fa","definition":"An excited activity; a stir.","example":"the hum and bustle; a din and bustle","pos":"noun","synonyms":["ado","hustle"],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"","register":"countable, uncountable","wordFamily":["bustlesome","bustly","hustle and bustle","hustle-bustle"],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bustle#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:ff38f60e72585736f5","definition":"A cover to protect and hide the back panel of a computer or other office machine.","example":"","pos":"noun","synonyms":[],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"","register":"countable","wordFamily":["bustlesome","bustly","hustle and bustle","hustle-bustle"],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bustle#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:5784dade090a8df236","definition":"A frame worn underneath a woman's skirt, typically only protruding from the rear as opposed to the earlier more circular hoops.","example":"A grotesque-looking lady wearing a bustle (Anna mentally undressed the woman, and was appalled at her hideousness), and a little girl laughing affectedly ran down the platform.","pos":"noun","synonyms":["tournure"],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"historical","register":"countable, historical","wordFamily":["bustlesome","bustly","hustle and bustle","hustle-bustle"],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bustle#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:2502d0cb2aceb932e4","definition":"Money; cash.","example":"Why the old clerical's turned coper—a new way of raising the wind——letting his friends down easy—gave you a good dinner, I suppose, Sir John, and took this method of drawing the bustle for it: an old trick of the reverend's.","pos":"noun","synonyms":[],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"UK, obsolete, slang","register":"UK, obsolete, slang, uncountable","wordFamily":["bustlesome","bustly","hustle and bustle","hustle-bustle"],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bustle#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:a077054ba001638f09","definition":"To move busily and energetically with fussiness (often followed by about).","example":"The commuters bustled about inside the train station.","pos":"verb","synonyms":["flit","hustle","scamper","scurry"],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"","register":"","wordFamily":["bustlesome","bustly","hustle and bustle","hustle-bustle","abustle","bustler","outbustle"],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bustle#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:bbc4b176aa23cbaf2d","definition":"To teem or abound (usually followed by with); to exhibit an energetic and active abundance (of a thing).","example":"The train station was bustling with commuters.","pos":"verb","synonyms":["abound","brim","bristle","burst","crawl","swell","teem"],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"","register":"","wordFamily":["bustlesome","bustly","hustle and bustle","hustle-bustle","abustle","bustler","outbustle"],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bustle#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:0659a2914900ed5efb","definition":"To push around, to importune.","example":"Don’t bustle her or fuss or snatch: / A suitor looking at his watch / Is not a posture that persuades / Willing, much less reluctant maids.","pos":"verb","synonyms":[],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"","register":"transitive","w